+// ---- property 15: a staged re-place under a session fence, a slow pass and a late write (#663) ----
+//
+// Reconstruction repairs a committed part's rotten fragment by a re-place: it pre-marks the
+// destination, writes the rebuilt fragment there under an authorization deadline fixed at the
+// pre-mark's stamp, and adopts it in one CAS pinned to the session still being `Open` at its epoch
+// and to the part record's exact bytes (`0016:825`). A Complete, an Abort or the reaper may fence
+// the session — its own CAS, `Open@E` to `Aborting@E+1` — at any instant. Whichever lands first, no
+// fragment may end unreferenced AND unevidenced: the written destination is either adopted by the
+// part record or covered by its pre-mark, which GC then acts on. And the session may never end
+// `Aborting` with its part record naming a fragment that was not written (`0016:888`).
+//
+// One clock runs the whole lifecycle: the testkit `SystemClock`, which madsim virtualises (its
+// wall clock starts at a seed-drawn instant and advances with simulated time). The pass reads its
+// `now_millis` from it, its `ReconstructionContext` carries it for the re-place's stamps, and the
+// D-server double judges the write's deadline on it exactly where the real D server does
+// (`ChunkStore::put_fragment`, #638): before it publishes — a write that sat in its accept queue
+// past the deadline is refused, and nothing lands — and after — a publication that ran past the
+// deadline cannot be certified (`Unknown`), and its bytes stay. The seed also makes the pass's
+// fragment reads slow: a pass that spends longer than a whole write window reading and assessing
+// must still stamp its pre-mark — and fix its deadline — as it writes the pre-mark, or every write
+// it sends is already expired and no staged repair ever lands.
+//
+// The per-leg cases in `crates/custodian/tests/staged_repair.rs` land each of these at one scripted
+// instant. Here the fence is a genuinely concurrent task over the simulated-TiKV model, landing
+// where the seed puts it, and every read, commit and D-server call the pass makes spans simulated
+// time — so the fence lands before the pre-mark, between the pre-mark and the write, between the
+// write and the adoption, or after it. The coverage leg proves all four are reached, that both of
+// the D server's refusals are, and that a slow pass still repairs.
+
+const REPLACE_UPLOAD: &str = "66306630663066306630663066306630";
+const REPLACE_OBJECT: &str = "replace";
+/// The committed part's chunk: `RS(2, 1)` on servers 0, 1, 2 (domains A, B, C).
+const REPLACE_CHUNK: ChunkId = 0x8050;
+/// The fragment the run rots: index 1, on server 1. Server 1 is kept busy, so the rebuild moves to
+/// domain D, server 3.
+const REPLACE_LOST: u16 = 1;
+const REPLACE_DEST: DServerId = 3;
+/// A D server's request and reply hops around a fragment write, in simulated milliseconds. Two
+/// each, so the write lands strictly between the pre-mark commit and the adoption's decision with
+/// whole milliseconds on both sides, where a fence can land without tying either.
+const REPLACE_WRITE_HOP: u64 = 2;
+/// How far into the run the fence is drawn, in simulated milliseconds. A fast pass here takes about
+/// 16: eight reads (the queue, the session listing, the owned and part ranges, the inodes, the
+/// vacated source's mark, the destination's drain record and its mark) of a hop each, the pre-mark
+/// commit (two hops), the write (two hops each way) and the adoption (two hops). The fence is a
+/// two-hop commit of its own, so this spans "before the pass lists the session" through "after the
+/// adoption".
+const REPLACE_SPAN: u32 = 20;
+/// The staged re-place's write window — the deployed one (`STAGED_REPAIR_WRITE_WINDOW_MILLIS`,
+/// `crates/server/src/cli.rs`): a write lands no later than this after its pre-mark's stamp.
+const REPLACE_WINDOW: u64 = 20_000;
+/// How long each fragment read of a SLOW pass takes: the gather's three reads outlast a whole write
+/// window before the pass reaches the pre-mark.
+const REPLACE_READ_STALL: u64 = REPLACE_WINDOW / 2;
+/// How long a late write sits in the D server's accept queue, or spends publishing: past its
+/// deadline either way.
+const REPLACE_LATE: u64 = REPLACE_WINDOW + 1_000;
